WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.834
Cancellation tokens are cooperative signals that let a .

2
00:00:03.834 --> 00:00:05.682
NET worker stop gracefully.

3
00:00:05.682 --> 00:00:15.952
They are especially important for services that may be processing files, queue messages, database updates, or external calls when the host shuts down.

4
00:00:15.952 --> 00:00:24.921
A worker that ignores cancellation may delay deployment, hold locks, abandon partial work, or make shutdown behavior unpredictable.

5
00:00:24.921 --> 00:00:28.207
Graceful shutdown should match the unit of work.

6
00:00:28.207 --> 00:00:32.794
Some operations can stop quickly and leave the job for later retry.

7
00:00:32.794 --> 00:00:41.831
Others need to finish a small critical section, record state, release a lease, close a file, or update a message visibility timeout.

8
00:00:41.831 --> 00:00:50.594
The worker should have a clear policy so operators know what happens during deployments, restarts, failures, and scaling events.

9
00:00:50.594 --> 00:00:52.854
Timeouts protect slow operations.

10
00:00:52.854 --> 00:01:03.055
Database queries, HTTP calls, storage operations, file processing, and long-running jobs should have reasonable limits based on the business process.

11
00:01:03.055 --> 00:01:10.449
Without timeouts, one dependency problem can tie up worker threads, connections, leases, and queue capacity.

12
00:01:10.449 --> 00:01:15.994
A timeout is not just an operations setting; it is a resource protection control.

13
00:01:15.994 --> 00:01:23.251
Concurrency limits, rate limits, batch sizes, queue prefetching, and bounded work protect shared capacity.

14
00:01:23.251 --> 00:01:25.853
More parallelism is not always better.

15
00:01:25.853 --> 00:01:34.000
A worker that consumes too many messages at once can overwhelm databases, downstream APIs, storage accounts, or memory.

16
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:42.284
Bounded processing lets teams reason about worst-case behavior and protects other services that share the same resources.

17
00:01:42.284 --> 00:01:48.514
Avoid unbounded loops, unbounded memory use, uncontrolled parallelism, and runaway retries.

18
00:01:48.514 --> 00:01:52.690
These patterns turn ordinary failures into broader incidents.

19
00:01:52.690 --> 00:02:02.412
Backpressure and load management help the worker slow down, reject work, delay work, or route failures when downstream systems cannot keep up.

20
00:02:02.412 --> 00:02:06.520
Long-running behavior should be observable and controllable.

21
00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:16.105
Operators should be able to see queue depth, active jobs, running duration, retry counts, cancellation events, resource use, and stuck work.

22
00:02:16.105 --> 00:02:23.636
They should also know how to pause, drain, restart, or scale workers safely when production conditions change.
